WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.364
A report is reading one row while a payment job updates it.

2
00:00:06.364 --> 00:00:10.649
With only one copy of the row, one of them has to wait for the other.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:15.883
Postgres avoids the wait by keeping more than one version of a row.

4
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:19.477
This is MVCC, multi-version concurrency control.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.463
Every version carries two hidden fields.

6
00:00:22.463 --> 00:00:25.226
xmin is the transaction that created it.

7
00:00:25.226 --> 00:00:29.853
xmax is the transaction that replaced it, or zero while nobody has.

8
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:33.920
Transaction 105 changes the balance to 450.

9
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.519
It doesn't overwrite the row.

10
00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:43.868
It writes a new version with xmin 105, and stamps 105 into the old version's xmax.

11
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.483
Each reader works from a snapshot: a note of which transactions had committed when it started.

12
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:57.637
Reader 1 took its snapshot while 105 was still running, so everything 105 wrote is invisible to it.

13
00:00:57.637 --> 00:01:00.764
It reads the old version, and sees 500.

14
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:03.707
Reader 2 starts after 105 commits.

15
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:09.107
For it, the old version has been replaced, and the new one is live.

16
00:01:09.107 --> 00:01:10.075
It sees 450.

17
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.202
Nobody waited.

18
00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:16.162
The writer made a new version instead of changing the one reader 1 was using.

19
00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:20.091
Readers don't block writers, and writers don't block readers.

20
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.492
When reader 1 finishes, no snapshot can see the old version anymore.

21
00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:28.313
It is a dead version, and it still takes up space in the page.

22
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:34.214
VACUUM finds versions that no snapshot can see, and marks their space free for new rows.

23
00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:37.571
Postgres also runs it on its own, in the background.

24
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.756
A version stays as long as any snapshot might still need it.

25
00:01:41.756 --> 00:01:46.965
So one transaction left open for hours stops VACUUM, and dead versions pile up.

26
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:55.365
So every update writes a new version, every snapshot picks the versions it may see, and VACUUM clears away the ones nobody can see.
